  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 346 PM MDT Thu May 28 2026\n\nSaturday will likely be another active day as the cut off low \nlocated to our west finally ejects off to the north and east over \nSoutheastern Wyoming and Western Nebraska. The associated vorticity \nmaximum along with 30-35 knot 500 mb flow rounding the base of the \nnegatively-tilted shortwave trough will provide a source for large-\nscale lift. Unlike the the previous day, this wave and associated \nspeed max will increase our vertical wind shear, with progged EBWD \nvalues of 25-30 knots primarily over the Nebraska Panhandle on \nSaturday afternoon. LREF guidance continues to show a theta-e tongue \ndriven by low-level SE upslope flow advect moisture towards portions \nof the Panhandle into the afternoon and evening hours, with an \nattendant dryline located further to the south over Southern \nNebraska, Eastern Colorado, and Kansas. Beneath this moisture axis, \nforecast soundings indicate a plume of modest (7-8 degC/km) 700-500 \nmb lapse rates superimposed atop upper 40F mid-50F surface \ndewpoints, resulting in MLCAPE values of 500-1000 J/Kg. As a result \nof the stout EML with 700 mb temperatures ranging from the 60-70th \npercentile relative to climatology, there is a somewhat of a cap to \novercome to achieve storm initiation. While these ingredients might \nhighly suggest severe weather for the High Plains of Nebraska, there \nare a couple of primary failure modes:\n\n1. Will the low-level moist, upslope flow result in a dense stratus \ndeck into the morning hours, therefore limiting heating and \ndestabilization into the afternoon hours? This decreases chances for \nsurface-based storm initiation given the presence of a cap.\n\n2. If there is little to no cloud cover early in the day, will the \nhigh May sun angle/strong surface heating rapidly mix out the low-\nlevel moisture, therefore reducing instability. \n\nThis second scenario is being shown on the NBM, which has surface \ndewpoints mixing down into the mid 40s by Saturday evening with \nsurface temperatures in the low 80s. This scenario would certainly \nfavor more of a damaging wind threat as opposed to large hail and \ntornadoes given inverted-v profiles and a dry sub-cloud layer. \nUltimately, the evolution of surface moisture, timing of the \nejection of the shortwave, and cloud cover will have to be monitored \ncloser to the event to get a better idea of the exact timing and \nhazard type(s).\n\nNow in true Wyoming fashion, it wouldn't be a forecast if we didn't \ninvolve high winds. As the shortwave trough lifts to the NE Saturday \nevening into the overnight hours, zonal 700 mb flow is expected to \nincrease to 45-50 knots over SE Wyoming combined with strong \nmountain-top static stability and associated downward omega \nsignatures, which all signal at a high wind event for our usual wind-\nprone areas, particularly Arlington. In-house random forest guidance \nhas high wind probabilities of 60% for Arlington Saturday overnight \ninto Sunday morning, with probabilities around 40% for Bordeaux.  \n\nOn Sunday, the flow becomes more zonal and 500 mb heights begin to \nrise as weak ridging builds in across our region, also yielding a \ndrop in PWAT. Values remain around the 45th percentile across our \narea per ensemble guidance, so expect to see a drop in shower and \nstorm coverage, with an activity being confined primarily to the \nSnowy and Sierra Madre Ranges. Thanks to rising heights and zonal \nflow, Sunday will be breezy with highs near or slightly above normal \n(average high for Cheyenne on May 31 is 71 degrees). Monday will be \nsimilar to Sunday, with a slight decrease in the winds and mostly \ndry conditions. Heading into the middle of the work week, the mid-\nlevel ridge begins to build across the Rocky Mountain West resulting \nin a gradual warming trend and mostly dry conditions, with no \nmeaningful and widespread precipitation in the extended. Ensembles \nare hinting at an increase in in low-level moisture as well as PWAT \nmainly for our Nebraska counties (nearing the 85th percentile), \nhowever these details will need to be sorted out in future updates.",
